@page "/have-a-plan"
<PageTitle>Have a plan · Tangent Atlas</PageTitle>

<div class="ta-container ta-page">
    <h1 class="ta-visually-hidden">Have a plan</h1>   @* The menu already names the page; the heading is kept for screen readers and search. *@
    <p class="ta-page-idea">What do you own?</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">A few hundred shares of one company. A few hundred of another. A big chunk in index funds. Cash spread out between three banks and a brokerage firm.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">But why, exactly, do you own this combination?</p>
    <p class="ta-plan-big">Do you have a plan?</p>
</div>

<section class="ta-band">
    <div class="ta-container ta-plan">
        <h2 class="ta-plan-turn">Tangent Atlas gives you one: your own personal index.</h2>
        <p class="ta-page-sub">Set to the level of risk you choose, without the companies you’d rather not own, and kept there by the same method the big index funds use.</p>
        <p class="ta-page-sub"><strong>Why buy a one-size-fits-all ETF when you can own the index yourself?</strong> Every share in your name, at your own broker. Every dividend straight to you.</p>

        <p class="ta-page-sub">Here is everything Ken owns.</p>

        <div class="ta-plan-example">
            <div class="ta-plan-name">Ken today</div>
            <div class="ta-plan-bar" role="img" aria-label="Ken today: JPM $60,000, DIS $40,000, SPY $200,000, QQQ $120,000, cash $80,000">
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c1" style="width: 12%"><b>JPM</b>$60k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c2" style="width: 8%"><b>DIS</b>$40k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c3" style="width: 40%"><b>SPY</b>$200k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c4" style="width: 24%"><b>QQQ</b>$120k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-cash" style="width: 16%"><b>Cash</b>$80k</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stats">
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Annual return</span><span class="ta-plan-value">10.4%</span></div>
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Volatility</span><span class="ta-plan-value">15.5%</span></div>
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Sharpe ratio</span><span class="ta-plan-value">0.48</span></div>
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Worst drop</span><span class="ta-plan-value">−26%</span></div>
            </div>
        </div>


        <div class="ta-plan-example">
            <div class="ta-plan-name">How much risk you take depends on how many companies you own</div>
            <svg class="ta-curve" viewBox="0 0 960 300" role="img" aria-label="Volatility falls from 52% a year for NVDA alone to 20% for the 30 largest companies and 17% for all 500.">
                <line x1="56" y1="252.0" x2="936" y2="252.0" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="46" y="256.0" text-anchor="end" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">0%</text><line x1="56" y1="174.7" x2="936" y2="174.7" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="46" y="178.7" text-anchor="end" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">20%</text><line x1="56" y1="97.3" x2="936" y2="97.3" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="46" y="101.3" text-anchor="end" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">40%</text><line x1="56" y1="20.0" x2="936" y2="20.0" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="46" y="24.0" text-anchor="end" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">60%</text>
                <path d="M56.0,51.3 L153.8,150.3 L251.6,151.5 L349.3,156.1 L447.1,169.3 L544.9,174.7 L642.7,178.5 L740.4,182.4 L838.2,185.1 L936.0,186.7 L936.0,252.0 L56.0,252.0 Z" fill="#E1F5EE" opacity="0.6"></path>
                <path d="M56.0,51.3 L153.8,150.3 L251.6,151.5 L349.3,156.1 L447.1,169.3 L544.9,174.7 L642.7,178.5 L740.4,182.4 L838.2,185.1 L936.0,186.7" fill="none" stroke="#2f5f8a" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
                <circle cx="56.0" cy="51.3" r="5" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><text x="68.0" y="55.3" font-size="13" fill="#1a1a1a">NVDA alone: 52%</text><circle cx="153.8" cy="150.3" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="251.6" cy="151.5" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="349.3" cy="156.1" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="447.1" cy="169.3" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="544.9" cy="174.7" r="7" fill="#0F6E56" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2"></circle><text x="544.9" y="158.7" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0F6E56">30 companies: 20%</text><circle cx="642.7" cy="178.5" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="740.4" cy="182.4" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="838.2" cy="185.1" r="4" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><circle cx="936.0" cy="186.7" r="5" fill="#2f5f8a"></circle><text x="936.0" y="172.7" text-anchor="end" font-size="13" fill="#1a1a1a">The whole index: 17%</text>
                <text x="56.0" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">1</text><text x="153.8" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">2</text><text x="251.6" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">5</text><text x="349.3" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">10</text><text x="447.1" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">20</text><text x="544.9" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">30</text><text x="642.7" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">50</text><text x="740.4" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">100</text><text x="838.2" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">200</text><text x="936.0" y="272.0" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">500</text>
                <text x="496.0" y="294" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#4b5563">Number of the largest S&amp;P 500 companies held</text>
            </svg>
            <p class="ta-figures">Volatility, % a year, backtested October 2021 to October 2026. One company: NVDA, as an example. Otherwise the largest S&amp;P 500 companies at each quarterly rebalancing date, weighted by the index method, fully invested.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="ta-page-sub">Now suppose Ken had chosen the 30 largest S&amp;P 500 companies as his own personal index, and kept more of his money in cash.</p>

        <div class="ta-plan-example">
            <div class="ta-plan-name">The Ken 30: Ken’s own personal index of the 30 largest S&amp;P 500 companies</div>
            <div class="ta-plan-bar" role="img" aria-label="The Ken 30: NVDA $47,000, AAPL $41,000, MSFT $33,000, 27 more companies $199,000, cash $180,000">
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c1" style="width: 9.4%"><b>NVDA</b>$47k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c3" style="width: 8.2%"><b>AAPL</b>$41k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-c2" style="width: 6.6%"><b>MSFT</b>$33k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-rest" style="width: 39.8%"><b>27 more</b>$199k</div>
                <div class="ta-plan-seg ta-plan-cash" style="width: 36%"><b>Cash</b>$180k</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stats ta-plan-stats-better">
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Annual return</span><span class="ta-plan-value">10.5%</span></div>
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Volatility</span><span class="ta-plan-value">12.8%</span></div>
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Sharpe ratio</span><span class="ta-plan-value">0.56</span></div>
                <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Worst drop</span><span class="ta-plan-value">−19%</span></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="ta-plan-big">The same return. Less risk. More cash.</p>
        <p class="ta-page-sub">We don’t promise the next five years will look like the last five. A plan isn’t a forecast. Tangent Atlas gives you the tools to manage your risk, build a diversified portfolio and see what a plan would have done for you. Then you decide. Having a plan beats not having one.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<div class="ta-container ta-page">
    <div class="ta-page-final">
        <p class="ta-page-close">Your index. Your shares. Your plan.</p>
        <AuthorizeView>
            <NotAuthorized>
                <a href="/signup" class="ta-btn ta-btn-primary ta-btn-large">Sign up</a>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </AuthorizeView>
    </div>
    <p class="ta-figures"><strong>About these figures.</strong> These are backtested results: a hypothetical illustration, not a prediction or a recommendation. They look backward, showing how each mix would have performed had it been held from October 22, 2021 to October 23, 2026. “Ken today” is an illustrative portfolio whose return and volatility sit at the median of 208,000 portfolios built from two S&amp;P 500 stocks held alongside SPY, QQQ and cash. The Ken 30 holds the 30 largest S&amp;P 500 companies as of each quarterly rebalancing date, weighted by the index method, with 36% in cash; that cash level was set so its return matched Ken today’s, and it is one of many possible settings. Ken’s 401(k) holds an S&amp;P 500 index fund, counted here with his SPY. Returns include dividends and exclude taxes, fees and trading costs. Volatility is annualized from daily returns; Sharpe ratios measure return above the risk-free rate for each unit of volatility; the worst drop is the largest fall from a peak. The Ken 30 holdings shown are approximate current weights. Past performance does not predict future results. All investing involves risk, including the loss of money you invest.</p>
</div>
